  <section class="tab-panel" data-panel="players" hidden>
    <h2>Players &amp; Regions</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      Who plays what. Both views are computed across a player's or a
      country's <em>entire</em> recorded Champions history, not a single
      event. Country codes are MunchStats' own two-letter codes, and a team
      only appears here if its player reported one.
    </p>
    <div class="subtabs" role="tablist">
      <button class="subtab-btn" role="tab" data-subtab="players-regions" aria-selected="true">By region</button>
      <button class="subtab-btn" role="tab" data-subtab="players-signature" aria-selected="false">By player</button>
    </div>

    <div class="subtab-panel" data-subpanel="players-regions">
      <div class="controls">
        <label>Country
          <select id="players-country-filter"></select>
        </label>
      </div>
      <p class="sub" id="players-country-note"></p>
      <div class="grid-6xn" id="players-country-grid"></div>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table id="players-country-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Rank</th><th>Pokémon</th><th class="sortable" data-sort-key="usage_share">Share of this country's picks</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtab-panel" data-subpanel="players-signature" hidden>
      <p class="sub">
        A player's most-fielded Pokémon across every team of theirs on record.
        Restricted to players with at least 2 recorded teams — a "signature"
        claim from someone with a single recorded team is just that team's
        roster. Only three Champions events exist anywhere, so two is a
        meaningful bar here rather than a low one: exactly one player in the
        whole dataset has three.
      </p>
      <div class="controls">
        <label>Player
          <select id="players-player-filter"></select>
        </label>
      </div>
      <p class="sub" id="players-player-note"></p>
      <div class="grid-6xn" id="players-signature-grid"></div>
      <h3>Most committed specialists</h3>
      <p class="sub">
        Players who brought their top pick to the largest share of their own
        recorded <em>teams</em> — 100% means it was on every team they
        fielded. Deliberately not ranked by share of roster slots: a Pokémon
        can only appear once per six-slot team, so that share is capped at
        16.7% and ties everyone who is fully committed.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table id="players-specialists-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Rank</th><th>Player</th><th>Country</th><th>Signature Pokémon</th><th class="sortable" data-sort-key="team_share">Share of their teams</th><th>Fielded in</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="tab-panel" data-panel="compare" hidden>
    <h2>Compare Pokémon</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Compare sourced competitive profiles side by side, then share the exact view with a URL.</p>
    <div class="filters">
      <label>Pokémon A <select id="compare-a"></select></label>
      <label>Pokémon B <select id="compare-b"></select></label>
      <button class="button" id="compare-copy" type="button">Copy view link</button>
    </div>
    <div id="compare-results"></div>
  </section>
  <section class="tab-panel" data-panel="core-explorer" hidden>
    <h2>Team Core Explorer</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Search observed pair and triple composition patterns, with sample size and lift kept visible.</p>
    <div class="filters">
      <label>Anchor Pokémon <select id="core-anchor"></select></label>
      <label>Core size <select id="core-size"><option value="pair">Pairs</option><option value="triple">Triples</option></select></label>
    </div>
    <div id="core-results"></div>
  </section>
  <section class="tab-panel" data-panel="data-sources" hidden>
    <h2>Data &amp; Sources</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      Where every number on this dashboard comes from, and whether it passed
      the release gates in <code>docs/dataset-spec.md</code>. Provenance is
      mandatory in this dataset — every record carries its source, URL,
      upstream record id and extraction timestamp — so it is reported here
      rather than left implicit.
    </p>

    <div class="subtabs" role="tablist">
      <button class="subtab-btn" role="tab" data-subtab="ds-sources" aria-selected="true">Sources</button>
      <button class="subtab-btn" role="tab" data-subtab="ds-gates" aria-selected="false">Release gates</button>
      <button class="subtab-btn" role="tab" data-subtab="ds-assets" aria-selected="false">Image coverage</button>
      <button class="subtab-btn" role="tab" data-subtab="ds-agreement" aria-selected="false">Source agreement</button>
      <button class="subtab-btn" role="tab" data-subtab="ds-history" aria-selected="false">History</button>
      <button class="subtab-btn" role="tab" data-subtab="ds-caveats" aria-selected="false">Caveats</button>
    </div>

    <div class="subtab-panel" data-subpanel="ds-sources">
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table id="ds-sources-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Endpoint</th><th>Availability</th><th class="sortable" data-sort-key="request_success_rate">Request success</th><th class="sortable" data-sort-key="rows_written">Rows</th><th class="sortable" data-sort-key="required_field_null_rate">Null rate</th><th>Checked</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtab-panel" data-subpanel="ds-gates" hidden>
      <p class="sub" id="ds-gates-status"></p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table id="ds-gates-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Gate</th><th>Check</th><th>What it measures</th><th>Threshold</th><th>Value</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtab-panel" data-subpanel="ds-assets" hidden>
      <p class="sub">
        How much of the imagery this dashboard references actually resolved.
        A Pokémon with no resolvable image degrades to its name in text —
        never a broken image — so a shortfall here is otherwise invisible.
      </p>
      <div class="grid-6xn" id="ds-assets-grid"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtab-panel" data-subpanel="ds-agreement" hidden>
      <p class="sub">
        Two independent sources publish Champions rosters — MunchStats and
        Limitless VGC. This is how often they agree, per event: what share of
        the players covered by both have an identical six-Pokémon roster, and
        what share of individual slots match. Disagreement is not
        necessarily either source being wrong (rosters get corrected, and
        coverage differs), but a sharp drop is worth knowing about before
        trusting a number downstream.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table id="ds-agreement-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Event</th><th class="sortable" data-sort-key="covered_players">Players covered by both</th><th class="sortable" data-sort-key="exact_agreement_rate">Exact roster agreement</th><th class="sortable" data-sort-key="slot_agreement_rate">Slot agreement</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtab-panel" data-subpanel="ds-history" hidden>
      <p class="sub" id="ds-history-status"></p>
      <h3>Retained extraction snapshots</h3>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table id="ds-snapshot-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th class="sortable" data-sort-key="snapshot_count">Snapshots</th><th>First date</th><th>Latest date</th><th class="sortable" data-sort-key="history_days">Days covered</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>Published releases</h3>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table id="ds-release-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Version</th><th class="sortable" data-sort-key="published_at_utc">Published</th><th class="sortable" data-sort-key="table_count">Tables</th><th>Images</th><th>Quality failures</th><th>Limitations</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subtab-panel" data-subpanel="ds-caveats" hidden>
      <p class="sub">
        Standing limitations that apply to figures elsewhere on this
        dashboard. Each is a property of what the sources publish, not a bug
        to be fixed here.
      </p>
      <ul class="caveat-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Head-to-head is team vs team, never Pokémon vs Pokémon.</strong>
          No source publishes a per-battle log naming which four of six were
          actually brought, so a "matchup" figure means teams containing A
          beat teams containing B — it is attributed to the whole roster.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Matchup appearance counts overstate sample roughly 6×</strong>
          for the same reason: every pairing is counted against all six
          Pokémon on each side.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The legal pool is an upper bound.</strong> PokéBase
          publishes no removal signal, so the cumulative legal-Pokémon count
          can only grow — a later-banned Pokémon is never subtracted.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Signature share is structurally capped at 16.7%</strong> —
          a Pokémon can appear only once on a six-slot team — which is why
          players are ranked by share of their <em>teams</em> instead.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Everything here is Champions-format only.</strong> The
          tournament corpus also covers standard VGC events; every usage and
          win-rate figure is filtered to Champions events first. Blending the
          two would be comparing different games.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No EV or IV spreads exist anywhere in this dataset.</strong>
          Official team sheets publish ability, held item, nature and moves
          and nothing more. The damage calculator therefore assumes a
          maximally-invested level-50 baseline on both sides rather than any
          real spread.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Legality has a single snapshot date</strong>, so nothing on
          this dashboard trends legality over time; usage trends are measured
          across tournament dates instead.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Champions stat deltas are all zero.</strong> No rebalance
          has been published yet, so every Pokémon's Champions stats still
          equal its canonical stats.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

